 ],
 "notes": "Method: parsed examside JEE Main chapter pages (all 32 Chemistry chapters, local copies of questions.examside.com/past-years/jee/jee-main/chemistry/<chapter>). They list 600 (2024), 475 (2025) and 474 (2026) questions, which matches every shift in full (20x30, 19x25, 19x25). I searched all 1,549 questions by keyword for every topic, whatever chapter tag they carried, then read the hits by hand. I opened about 25 individual question pages to see the options. I did no MathonGo/PW cross-check, because the examside list is complete by count. Counting rule: a question counts when the Adv-only content is needed to answer it, including one statement in a multi-statement or match-list question. It does not count when the same fact is taught in a Main unit: VSEPR shapes, group trends, salt-analysis tests on Main ions, Dumas/Kjeldahl as such.\nVERDICT ON THE 5 'REMOVED BUT TAGGED' CHAPTERS: (1) Surface Chemistry, 2 in 2024 (Jan 31 S1 'adsorption principle used in which purification method'; Apr 4 S2 'adsorbents in adsorption chromatography: silica gel/alumina...'): both are chromatography from Main's Purification unit. Not surface chemistry, so 0. (2) Chemistry in Everyday Life, 2 in 2024 (Feb 1 S2 and Apr 6 S1, uses of CCl4/CH2Cl2/iodoform/freons/DDT): this is the polyhalogen-compounds section of Main's Haloalkanes unit. Not drugs or soaps, so 0. (3) s-Block, 1 in 2024 (Apr 6 S2, flame emission wavelengths of Li/Na/Rb/Cs): this is genuine s-block content (NCERT flame colours), but none of the 7 listed s-block topics covers it, so it is noted here, not counted. (4) States of Matter, 1 in 2025 (Apr 7 S1, partial pressures of N2/O2/Ar in air from mass %): this is genuinely Dalton's law and is counted. (The van der Waals 'a' ordering question that an earlier mis-aligned parse put in 2025 is really 2023 Apr 8.) (5) Metallurgy, 1 in 2025 (Jan 28 S2, 'purification method based on solid->vapour->solid', i.e. sublimation): this is organic purification. Not metallurgy, so 0. Note: the earlier lead's text was off by one (each label was attached to the next question); fixed here.\nBIGGEST HIDDEN RISKS: Faraday's laws (6/3/1). They are not named in the NTA text but are asked almost every year in the Electrochemistry chapter. Gas laws and Dalton's law are needed inside Main questions (4 Dumas-method numericals in 2025, Kp and thermodynamics numericals). Sulphur compounds (3/2/2). Anomers and glycosidic linkages (2/3/3). E(M3+/M2+) of 3d metals (4/2/1). Kolbe and phenol-Zn (2/0/1). Metal carbonyls (2/0/1). Mn2+ group IV analysis (2/0/1).\nNOT COUNTED BUT RELEVANT: VSEPR/hybridisation questions on Xe compounds (XeF2, XeF4, XeO3, XeOF4, XeO2F2) number 2/3/8 in 2024/25/26. Those on interhalogens and polyhalides (ClF3, BrF5, IF7, ICl4-, I3-) number 10/5/7. They sit in Chemical Bonding, and the shapes follow from VSEPR (Main), so the student needs to know these formulas exist even if he skips the p-block sections. Ni(CO)4 VBT/magnetism questions (1/5/5) were also left out of 'Metal carbonyls'. Acid/basic/amphoteric/neutral nature of CO, SiO2, N2O, NO, SnO2, PbO2 came up in about 4 questions (2024 x3, 2026 x1), treated as Main group trends. Main salt-analysis tests (brown ring NO, Nessler NH3, chromyl chloride) were left out of the N/Cl topics. CaCO3/MgCO3 ignition stoichiometry (2024 Jan 31 S2) and CO2 + Ca(OH)2 (2025 Jan 22 S1) need only Class-10 facts, so they are not in s-block. Blast-furnace reactions given in equilibrium/stoichiometry questions (2024 Apr 5 S1, 2025 Jan 24 S1) need no metallurgy. H2O2 as a disproportionation example (2024 Jan 30 S2, 2025 Jan 28 S1) can be done from oxidation states. 2025 Apr 3 S1 'homogeneous catalyst cannot change equilibrium composition' needs only Main kinetics/equilibrium.\nCAVEATS: borderline judgements carry confidence 'medium' or 'low'. Examples: one statement among several (2024 Jan 30 S1 counted for Al electrolysis, cyanide leaching and EDTA stability), gas-law use inside thermodynamics/equilibrium questions, cellulose/nylon counted under Polymers although the questions are tagged Biomolecules/Bonding. Some topics overlap Main units (enzymes, globular proteins, cyanide leaching), so a nonzero count there does not by itself mean the student would miss the question. Examples use S1 = morning, S2 = evening shift."
}